# Copy to .env and edit. .env is git-ignored; this file is not.
#
# There is no secret in here and none may ever be added. The two credentials
# this project uses are FILES on the host, referred to by directory below, and
# they are never passed through the environment: `docker inspect` and
# /proc/1/environ both expose a container's environment, which is why the relay
# reads its key from a file rather than a variable.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets directory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to a directory OUTSIDE this repository holding two files:
#
#   game-credential   two lines: the character name, then its password
#   openrouter.key    the OpenRouter API key, one line
#
# The DIRECTORY is the access control here, not the file mode. Create it 0700
# so no other host user can traverse it, and leave the files inside readable:
#
#   mkdir -p -m 700 ~/.config/dikumud-hermes
#   printf '%s\n%s\n' 'YourCharacter' 'the-password' \
#     > ~/.config/dikumud-hermes/game-credential
#   chmod 644 ~/.config/dikumud-hermes/game-credential
#
# 0644 on a credential looks wrong and is deliberate. Outside swarm, Compose
# bind-mounts a file secret with the host file's ownership and mode and does
# not remap it, so a 0600 file owned by the operator is unreadable to a service
# running as its own uid: the relay crash-loops with "No OpenRouter credential
# available". The alternative is to run each service as the file's owner, which
# breaks the dedicated non-root identity every service is required to keep.
# With the directory at 0700 no other host user can reach the file, and the
# Docker daemon could read it at
# any mode regardless.
#
# Compose does not expand ~, so write the path in full.
SECRETS_DIR=/home/YOUR-USER/.config/dikumud-hermes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Game character
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must match the first line of the game-credential file. The name is not
# secret; the password beside it is, which is why only the name appears here.
#
# To CONTINUE a character, leave this and the credential file alone. The
# character's own learning is stored under its name, so it picks up its notes
# where it left them.
#
# To start a NEW character, put a new name and password in the credential file
# and set this to match. On the next connect the game will not know the name,
# mud-control answers the identity prompts (name, confirmation, new password,
# retype), and then stops: sex and class are the agent's to choose through
# mud_act, one command at a time, which is what E2E-01 demonstrates. The new
# character begins with an empty learning store, because it has not played yet.
MUD_CHARACTER=Wren

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pinned models
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The models the relay may request, and the order it tries them in, live in
# config/relay/models.toml, which compose mounts read-only into the relay. That
# file is where models are set; there is no model identifier anywhere in the
# relay's source. `scripts/set-model-order` changes the order without editing
# it by hand, and keeps the agent's profile in step.
#
# Set this to keep the file outside the repository, beside your secrets. It is
# a path on the host, and compose does not expand ~, so write it in full.
#
#RELAY_MODEL_CONFIG_FILE=/home/YOUR-USER/.config/dikumud-hermes/models.toml
#
# Three things worth knowing before editing the file:
#
#   - The relay reads the file once, at startup. A change takes effect on
#     `docker compose up -d --force-recreate openrouter-relay` and never in the
#     middle of a session.
#   - It fails closed. A file that is missing, unparseable or invalid stops the
#     relay with the reason in `docker compose logs openrouter-relay`; it never
#     falls back to the set packaged in the image.
#   - Substituting a model is not just an id. Work through the capability
#     checklist in public-documentation/OPERATIONS.md section 12 first: a model
#     that cannot emit a tool call cannot play the game at all, and the failure
#     looks like an agent that talks instead of acting.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model fallback
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The relay tries the configured models in the order the file gives. These
# knobs tune how long it waits on each; they cannot add, remove or reorder
# models, and they cannot let a caller choose one. Reordering is
# `scripts/set-model-order`.
#
# WORST CASE FOR ONE TURN, which is what these two knobs actually set:
#
#     (ordered models - 1) * RELAY_PRIMARY_TIMEOUT + RELAY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT
#
# At the defaults below that is 165s for two models and 210s for the three a
# clean clone ships. RELAY_PRIMARY_TIMEOUT=25 brings three back to about 170s,
# and 25s still sits above the 21s p95 observed for the largest of them, so a
# merely slow model is still used. Set it if turns feel long.
#
# RELAY_FALLBACK=0 disables the second attempt entirely and restores the
# original fail-closed-on-first-failure behaviour. Not recommended for a
# demonstration run.
#
# RELAY_PRIMARY_TIMEOUT is the wall-clock deadline on every attempt but the
# last, in seconds. It exists because an unavailable free endpoint does not
# refuse: it accepts the connection and holds it open, sending keep-alive
# bytes that reset any socket read timeout. Only a wall-clock bound catches
# that. Keep it above the primary's normal latency (p95 21s, max 57s observed)
# or a healthy-but-slow primary will be abandoned unnecessarily.
#
# RELAY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT is the deadline on the last attempt, which keeps the
# full budget because there is nothing left to fall back to.
#
# RELAY_MAX_RATE_WAIT is how long a request may wait for a rate-limit slot
# before being refused (default 30s). The relay allows 20 requests a minute
# because that is OpenRouter's published cap for free model variants,
# and it is not ours to raise: a higher RELAY_MAX_RPM only moves the refusal to
# the endpoint. A fast model spends that minute in well under a minute, so the
# relay paces requests instead of failing them, which means one request every
# three seconds when the window is full.
#
# Set it to 0 to refuse a rate-limited request immediately instead of pacing it.
#
#RELAY_MAX_RATE_WAIT=30
#
# Worst case a caller waits is RELAY_PRIMARY_TIMEOUT + RELAY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT.
#
#RELAY_FALLBACK=1
#RELAY_PRIMARY_TIMEOUT=45
#RELAY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT=120

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image references
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three built images are tagged locally. For a release, replace each tag
# with the digest of the image you built and archived, so a redeploy cannot
# silently pick up a different build:
#
#   docker image inspect dikumud:phase7 --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}'
#
DIKUMUD_TAG=phase7
MUD_CONTROL_TAG=phase7
RELAY_TAG=phase7

# The agent image is upstream and is always used by digest, never by tag.
# This is the pinned revision from public-documentation/DEPENDENCY_RECORD.md
# (nousresearch/hermes-agent:v2026.8.18). Changing it requires re-running the
# tool-surface inventory, because a built-in toolset Hermes has not seen before
# arrives ENABLED.
HERMES_DIGEST=sha256:22e37bb4ed1b0f50cb6bd991dca7ecacd6c9f29df9b4a20fc989d32bc763ccf6
